        "why": "The three zones are defined by depth and by how far the light reaches, so a drawing settles in one picture what a list of names cannot: the littoral zone is shallow and at the edge, the limnetic zone is the lit open water, and the profundal zone is the dark water beneath it.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Drawing the three zones as separate boxes. They are bands of one body of water and must touch.",
          "Putting the profundal zone at the shore. It is the DEEP zone, below the limnetic zone.",
          "Leaving out the benthic region. The lake floor is a named part of the figure."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Draw the bowl of the lake first, then cut it with two lines: one down from the shore, one across at the light limit.",
        "margin_note": "Label the three zones, the benthic region and the light limit. The labels carry the two marks."
      },
      {
        "id": "s3_zones",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "The three zones",
        "marks": 2,
        "mark_note": "Zones",
        "lines": [
          "1) Littoral zone: the shallow part of the",
          "lake, close to the shore. Light reaches",
          "right down to its bottom, so it is the",
          "euphotic or good light zone. It has rich",
          "vegetation, a high rate of photosynthesis",
          "and is rich in oxygen.",
          "2) Limnetic zone: the central, largest and",
          "open zone, away from the shore. It does not",
          "touch the bottom. It extends down to the",
          "level of effective light penetration.",
          "The zone of compensation is its lower",
          "limit, where the rate of photosynthesis is",
          "equal to the rate of respiration.",
          "3) Profundal zone: the deep water zone below",
          "the limnetic zone. Light is absent, so",
          "photosynthesis does not take place, and it",
          "is poor in oxygen."
        ],
        "why": "The three zones are separated by ONE thing — how much light reaches them — so each is described by its light first and by its oxygen second; that order makes the fall in oxygen from top to bottom follow from the light.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that the limnetic zone reaches the bottom. It does not touch the lake floor.",
          "Leaving out the zone of compensation, which is the named boundary between the lit and dark water.",
          "Writing that the profundal zone is rich in oxygen. Without photosynthesis it is POOR in oxygen."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Follow the light down: full light, fading light, no light. The oxygen falls with it.",
        "margin_note": "One paragraph per zone with its light and its oxygen. This is two marks, so do not shorten it."
      },
      {
        "id": "s4_flora",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Flora of the littoral zone",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Flora",
        "lines": [
          "The littoral zone holds the richest biota.",
          "Flora — plants, with examples:",
          "i) Free floating hydrophytes — Pistia,",
          "Lemna, Salvinia",
          "ii) Rooted hydrophytes with floating leaves",
          "— Nymphaea, Victoria regia",
          "iii) Submerged suspended hydrophytes —",
          "Hydrilla, Utricularia",
          "iv) Submerged rooted hydrophytes —",
          "Vallisneria",
          "v) Amphibious plants, rooted below the water",
          "with the shoot above it — Sagittaria, Typha",
          "vi) Phytoplankton — diatoms, green algae"
        ],
        "why": "The question asks for examples by name, so the plants are grouped by HOW they sit in the water — floating, rooted, submerged, amphibious — and each group carries its own named example, which is the form the marks are given in.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing the plant names as one list with no groups. The grouping is what is marked.",
          "Putting Vallisneria with the free floating plants. It is submerged and ROOTED."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Six groups by position: floating on top, rooted with floating leaves, suspended, rooted below, half in half out, and the drifting phytoplankton.",
        "margin_note": "Six groups, each with its examples. A list scores as fast as sentences, so use the dash form."
      },
      {
        "id": "s5_fauna",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Fauna of the littoral zone",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Fauna",
        "lines": [
          "Fauna — animals, with examples:",
          "i) Zooplankton, the drifting animals —",
          "Daphnia (water flea), rotifers, ostracods",
          "ii) Neuston, epineuston, living on the water",
          "surface — water bugs, beetles",
